Parity gap: the Parity Principle (AUR-CANONICAL-001 §VIII) requires the
external Node MCP decision core and the in-process Python twin to produce
bit-for-bit identical decisions for identical inputs. That is a stated
requirement, not an automatically guaranteed property. parity/run_parity.py
compares the two on a fixed set of golden vectors, and
.github/workflows/parity.yml runs it in CI against gate_core.js checked out
from br-collab/Cato-FICC-MCP; the Node core is still not vendored here. The
comparison covers only what the vectors exercise.

One divergence is known and unrepaired. Since Cato v0.3.0 the Node chain
picker prefers XRPL when its fee is under $0.01; the Python twin has no XRPL
branch. On the same chain state (XRPL fee $0.00003, Solana $0.0004, Base
0.01 gwei, Ethereum 0.5 gwei) Node recommends xrpl and the twin recommends
solana. Golden vector V17 records this as KNOWN-FAILING and is reported on
every run; the mirror spec is PARITY_XRPL.md in br-collab/Cato-FICC-MCP, and
the work to land it is tracked in https://github.com/br-collab/aureon/issues/9.

A second divergence is in the inputs, not the decision core. The Node gate
reads FRED STLFSI4; the Python twin and the pre-trade policy engine read
the OFR Financial Stress Index — scraped from financialresearch.gov, or,
when that fails, a proxy computed from VIX, high-yield spreads and the
yield curve. The golden vectors supply identical inputs to both sides, so
parity holds under test; in production the two are fed different series,
and the 0.5/1.0 thresholds were backtested on STLFSI4 only. Tracked in
https://github.com/br-collab/aureon/issues/11.
